-- Pour-rate starting point (~5 g/s), not a reproduction of this source's recipe:
-- https://coffeeadastra.com/2020/05/23/the-physics-of-kettle-streams/

= Prep =
Rinse the filter, warm the brewer, then discard the rinse water. Add @coffee{20%g} to the #V60{} and make a small well in the center.

= Bloom =
Wet all the grounds with @water{60%g} over about ~scaled{12%seconds}, then give the brewer a gentle swirl.

= Let it bloom =
Let the coffee release its gas. No water needed. Wait until ~until{45%seconds} on the brew clock.

= Pour 1 =
Pour @water{86 2/3%g} over about ~scaled{17%seconds} in steady circles, from the center outward. Aim for a comfortable stream, not an exact rate. Avoid the filter walls.

= Let it settle =
Give the water time to drain through the bed for ~{20%seconds}.

= Pour 2 =
Pour @water{86 2/3%g} over about ~scaled{17%seconds} in steady circles, from the center outward. Aim for a comfortable stream, not an exact rate. Avoid the filter walls.

= Let it settle =
Give the water time to drain through the bed for ~{20%seconds}.

= Pour 3 =
Pour @water{86 2/3%g} over about ~scaled{17%seconds} in steady circles, from the center outward. Aim for a comfortable stream, not an exact rate. Avoid the filter walls.

= Draw down =
Gently swirl to level the bed. Allow about ~{74%seconds} for the remaining water to drain. Finish when drained, not at a fixed clock time.

= Finish =
Swirl, sip, and tell your journal how it went.
